science certainly affects everything we  do but it's also true that we affect the  scientific process  imagine that science and society  communicate through a game of telephone  information is passed from hand to hand  or institution to institution  you see scientists publish research  papers which eventually reach  policymakers who then apply the  knowledge in ways that benefit the world  right  well sort of this picture gets  complicated once you realize that  society creates a climate of social  economic and political factors that  influence scientists and policymakers  the linear game of telephone becomes a  loop and the scientific message is more  likely to become distorted here we can  show you how this loop is formed by  telling the story of asbestos mining in  South Africa  wait wait wait you've probably heard of  asbestos but do you really know what it  is asbestos is a mineral that's stronger  than steel inflammable sound absorbent  and flexible which makes it an  economically important resource between  1910 and 2002 South Africa was the  world's third largest exporter of  asbestos during that time South Africa  turned a huge profit mining more than 10  million tons of it that's equivalent to  almost 28 Empire State buildings but  let's take a closer look at the social  context in 20th century South Africa  life was divided along racial lines of  apartheid the government enforced  segregation of public spaces medical  care even citizenship with limited  employment opportunities many black  South Africans turned to manual work  like asbestos mining entire families  would work together to drill collect and  separate asbestos from the earth but  asbestos as it turns out is particularly  dangerous to mine although asbestos may  look like solid rock when it's drilled  and blasted by miners it can unravel  into millions of small fibers which  people can breathe in normally when a  foreign substance enters the lungs it  gets digested into mucus and coughed up  but asbestos dust builds up over time  eventually causing inflammation and the  four  Meishan of scar tissue daily exposure to  asbestos may cause serious respiratory  problems that appear later in life  illnesses can lay dormant for decades  within the body such as mesothelioma  which are cancerous tumors that form in  vital organs families of mine workers  knew they were getting sick from  asbestos exposure but the industry  ignored their protests as more people  found out about the dangers of asbestos  how long do you think it took for mines  to start implementing workplace safety  regulations in 1959 scientists presented  extensive research demonstrating that  mesothelioma was unusually common in  South African miners and that even brief  exposure to asbestos could cause  symptoms to appear  it wasn't until 1986 that mining  companies started negotiating with  miners to improve workplace safety it  took over a quarter of a century for  this science to be implemented into  policy what happened here to caused such  a delay between science and action well  remember how society shapes the game of  telephone into a loop between 1959 and  1986 South Africa's economic interests  favoured asbestos mining further  distorting the scientific message  the 1959 publication prompted the mining  industry to fund research on the health  effects of asbestos but this research  wasn't at all designed to examine the  link between workplace conditions and  lung disease for example one study even  attempted to link mesothelioma to a  polio vaccine that most South Africans  hadn't even received meanwhile the mines  continued business as usual while black  laborers protested their unsafe working  conditions in the mid 70s and early 80s  the asbestos trade slowed as both global  and domestic economic pressure against  apartheid increased in 1986 after a  national anti asbestos campaign and  several more strikes the first health  and safety requirements for the asbestos  mining industry were finally put into  place  in the end it took a perfect storm of  social political and economic factors  for science done in the lab to directly  benefit workers in the mine today  scientific discoveries are made at an  accelerated rate all of the new  information goes through a similar  game of telephone the story of his  bestest mining shows us that while  society depends on informed science  scientists also need reminding of social  contexts a study in 2008 for example  showed that carbon nanotubes have a  fiber shape similar to asbestos when  mice were exposed to these nanotubes  they developed mesothelioma like  symptoms billions of dollars are funding  nanotechnology research and carbon  nanotubes can already be found in a  variety of products from sunscreen to  eyeglasses in short scientists have  introduced a promising material but  ongoing research points to potentially  harmful effects of its use sound  familiar  science is a series of social  interactions and especially when it  comes to health any conflicting messages  in the conversation can have serious  consequences people such as science  writers professors and citizen  scientists work against the likelihood  of these miscommunications moving  forward it is important to shorten the  gap between scientists and the public to  fortify the imperfect loop and keep  scientific messages connected to their  social contexts  you
 

